  "blocks": [
    {
      "kind": "markdown",
      "text": "*Author: Byron*\n\n*Subject: outline an event model for an autonomous stage executor*"
    },
    {
      "kind": "context",
      "context_id": "context:namespace:trifecta.stage:1"
    },
    {
      "kind": "markdown",
      "text": "`namespace trifecta.stage`"
    },
    {
      "kind": "context",
      "context_id": "context:class:Actor:1"
    },
    {
      "kind": "markdown",
      "text": "## Actor\n\nTo perform a sequence of Scene comprising a stage for some duration 100ms <= tx <= 5s, while responding to\nchanges in conditions, an autonomous Actor will operate on its own threaed and coordinate events under a\nsingle instance-specific lock. All actions will be trivial except when performing a Scene that involves\nsignificant continuous computation. Responsiveness to concurrent events during computation will depend on\ngranularity of the computation steps; for example, if a Scene performs computation in 10ms loop iterations,\nthen events relevant to the Actor may go unnoticed for as long as 10ms. For a Scene that consumes one or\nmore resources and does not perform computation, it is assumed that the Actor remains passively available\nfor event notification while each resource performs work on s separate driver thread.\n\nWhen not performing computation, an Actor is either busy with a trivial event while holding its lock, or\nwaits on its lock until some notification occurs. Implementation can most likely use a simple Monitor,\nwhich can be held privately within Actor. Events will arrive over interface callbacks, which Actor can\nimplement as synchronizing on its Monitor. Before initiating a wait phase, Actor will discover any state\nchanges on subscribed event sources; if found, no wait will occur and Actor will directly respond.\n\n### Data Structure"
    },
    {
      "kind": "type",
      "type_id": "type:trifecta.stage.Actor"
    },
    {
      "kind": "context",
      "context_id": "context:class:Appearance:1"
    },
    {
      "kind": "markdown",
      "text": "## Appearance\n\nEach time an Actor queues for resources it presents a fresh Appearance, which is what the resource queues hold\nand call back. When an Actor leaves a queue (for example, on stop) it simply discards its Appearance; a queue that\nlater finds a stale Appearance recognizes it as a ghost and skips it, without needing to search the queue.\n\n### Data Structure"
    },
    {
      "kind": "type",
      "type_id": "type:trifecta.stage.Appearance"
    },
    {
      "kind": "context",
      "context_id": "context:class:Scene:1"
    },
    {
      "kind": "markdown",
      "text": "## Scene\n\n### Data Structure"
    },
    {
      "kind": "type",
      "type_id": "type:trifecta.stage.Scene"
    },
    {
      "kind": "context",
      "context_id": "context:class:Director:1"
    },
    {
      "kind": "markdown",
      "text": "## Director\n\nAttempts to optimize productivity by regulating the number of concurrent actors.\n\n### Data Structure"
    },
    {
      "kind": "type",
      "type_id": "type:trifecta.stage.Director"
    },
    {
      "kind": "context",
      "context_id": "context:class:CastOptimizer:1"
    },
    {
      "kind": "markdown",
      "text": "## CastOptimizer"
    },
    {
      "kind": "type",
      "type_id": "type:trifecta.stage.CastOptimizer"
    },
    {
      "kind": "markdown",
      "text": "## Interfaces"
    },
    {
      "kind": "type",
      "type_id": "type:trifecta.stage.ICastManager"
    },
    {
      "kind": "markdown",
      "text": "## Enumerations"
    },
    {
      "kind": "type",
      "type_id": "type:trifecta.stage.SceneStatus"
    },
    {
      "kind": "type",
      "type_id": "type:trifecta.stage.ActorPhase"
    }
  ],
